WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.860
MFA can reduce account takeover risk, but it must be designed with usability, recovery, and enrollment security in mind.

2
00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:15.240
An application that adds MFA but leaves weak reset and support flows may simply move attackers to the easier path.

3
00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:28.940
Enrollment, device changes, MFA reset, backup codes, remembered devices, and step-up prompts should be logged, reviewed, and tested because they affect the strength of the overall authentication system.

4
00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:34.020
Recovery flows are authentication flows and should be treated as high risk.

5
00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:45.880
Password reset, email change, device enrollment, account deletion, administrator invitation, role change, and MFA reset can all change who controls an account.

6
00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:56.700
Recovery links, one-time codes, and temporary credentials should be short-lived, single-use where appropriate, and protected from logs and unnecessary URL exposure.

7
00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:04.080
High-risk events may require reauthentication or stronger verification before the action completes.

8
00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:13.320
Lifecycle controls should cover account disablement, lockout, session revocation, credential rotation, support procedures, and incident response.

9
00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:18.300
A user whose password is reset may need existing sessions invalidated.

10
00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:22.320
A disabled account should not continue using refresh tokens.

11
00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:26.820
A role change may require rechecking authorization in active sessions.

12
00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:33.640
The application should make these transitions explicit rather than treating account state as something checked only at login time.
